          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 11614.38903214987,
            "y": 16957.070093175585
          },
          "code": "FIN_IEX_LLM_DISPLAY_RESPONSE_FOR_ALL_VALID_ACTIONABLE_REQUESTS",
          "id": "1777820898380",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1777820898380_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Display a success message in a `cardWidget` based on the intent code `{{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_CUSTOMER_DETAILS.$output.result.intentCode}}`.\n\n### Objective\nGenerate only the `cardWidget` JSON for a success message.\n\n### Required Output Shape\n \nConstruct only the `oraInfoDisplay` object in this shape:\n<oraInfoDisplay key=\"{{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_CUSTOMER_DETAILS.$output.result.messageId}}\">\n{\n  \"patternId\": \"cardWidget\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"subject\": \"<intent-based subject>\",\n    \"summary\": \"<intent-based one-sentence success message>\"\n  }\n}\n</oraInfoDisplay>\n### Mandatory Structure Rules\n- Include only these top-level fields:\n  - `patternId`\n  - `properties`\n- Set `patternId` exactly to `cardWidget`.\n- Put all display values only inside `properties`.\n- Put the success header only in `properties.subject`.\n- Put the body text only in `properties.summary`.\n- Do not include `title`.\n- Do not include `description`.\n- Do not include `subtitle`.\n- Do not include `header`.\n- Do not include `body`.\n- Do not include any extra fields, buttons, actions, metadata, wrappers, keys, tags, or explanations.\n- Return the json with `<oraInfoDisplay>` tags.\n- Do not return markdown or code fences.\n- Return only the final widget JSON.\n\n### Content Rules\n- Render the message in only one place.\n- Show the success label only in `properties.subject`.\n- Do not repeat the subject text inside `properties.summary`.\n- Keep the body message to exactly one sentence.\n- Do not mention the intent code in the output.\n\n### Intent-Based Subject and Summary Rules\n- If the intent code is `ORA_PROMISE_TO_PAY`:\n  - Set `properties.subject` exactly to: `Promise Recorded`\n  - Set `properties.summary` exactly to: `The promise has been recorded in the system successfully.`\n- If the intent code is `ORA_DISPUTE_REQUEST`:\n  - Set `properties.subject` exactly to: `Dispute Request Registered`\n  - Set `properties.summary` exactly to: `The dispute request has been registered in the system successfully.`\n- If the intent code is `ORA_PO_UPDATE`:\n  - Set `properties.subject` exactly to: `PO Number Updated`\n  - Set `properties.summary` exactly to: `The purchase order number has been updated successfully.`\n- If the intent code is `ORA_INVOICE_REPRINT`:\n  - Set `properties.subject` exactly to: `Invoice Copies Resent`\n  - Set `properties.summary` exactly to: `The invoice copies have been resent successfully.`\n\n### Good Example\n<oraInfoDisplay key=\"{{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_CUSTOMER_DETAILS.$output.result.messageId}}\">\n{\n  \"patternId\": \"cardWidget\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Dispute Request Registered\",\n    \"summary\": \"The dispute request has been registered in the system successfully.\"\n  }\n}\n</oraInfoDisplay>\n### Bad Examples\nBad because it includes forbidden top-level fields:\n{\n  \"title\": \"Success Message\",\n  \"patternId\": \"cardWidget\",\n  \"description\": \"Success message for dispute request registration\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Dispute Request Registered\",\n    \"summary\": \"The dispute request has been registered in the system successfully.\"\n  }\n}\n\nReturn only the final widget JSON object."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_MULTIRECORD"
              ]
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Displaying Processing status for failed promise requests",
            "name": "Display Processing Status for Failed Promise Requests"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 9365.102779737128,
            "y": 17338.977478457964
          },
          "code": "FIN_IEX_CODE_DISPLAY_PROCESSING_STATUS_FOR_FAILED_PROMISE_REQUESTS",
          "id": "1777822428149",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1777822428149_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Generate a multirecord widget displaying promise-to-pay details with column labels: Invoice Number, Invoice Date, Due Date, Amount Remaining, Currency, Promise Amount, Promise Date, Status, Error Details, Recommended Action.\n\nEach row should show the following fields: BusinessUnit, BillToCustomerAccount, TransactionNumber, TransactionDate, InstallmentDueDate, TransactionAmountRemaining, TransactionCurrency, PromiseAmount, PromiseDate, processingStatusMeaning, Error Details, and Recommended Action.\n\nMap Status from `processingStatusMeaning`.\n\nMap Error Details conditionally:\n- display Error Details only when `processingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`\n- when `processingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, populate Error Details from summarized response data\n- you may rewrite, clean up, and simplify the original error message if needed so that it is clearer and more user-friendly\n- display the error details correctly and concisely\n- do not include internal error codes such as `IEX-XXXXXX`\n- if the error message contains timestamps, do not mention the time portions in date-related details unless they are truly necessary\n- prefer clean business-friendly wording over raw system wording\n- otherwise display an empty value for Error Details\n\nMap Recommended Action conditionally:\n- display Recommended Action only when `processingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`\n- when `processingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, generate a concise recommendation based on the corrected error message\n- the recommendation should help the user understand how to correct the data and process the promise manually\n- keep the recommendation practical, specific, and user-friendly\n- otherwise display an empty value for Recommended Action\n\nUse the formatted data from the upstream code node: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_IEX_CODE_PROCESS_FAILED_PROMISE_REQUESTS.$output.result}}. Return only the widget JSON."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_MULTIRECORD"
              ]
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Displaying Processing status for failed promise requests",
            "name": "Display Processing Status for Failed Dispute Requests"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 10006.870441395806,
            "y": 17339.578645236397
          },
          "code": "FIN_IEX_LLM_DISPLAY_PROCESSING_STATUS_FOR_FAILED_DISPUTE_REQUESTS",
          "id": "1778388759003",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1777822428149_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Generate `oraInfoDisplay` as a multiRecordWidget for dispute details using only this upstream JSON as the source of truth:\n{{$context.$nodes.FIN_IEX_CODE_PROCESS_FAILED_DISPUTE_REQUESTS.$output.result}}\n\nInstructions:\n1. Return only valid output. Do not include markdown, explanations, comments, or code fences.\n1a. All generated summary or guidance text must appear only inside the `description` field of the `<oraInfoDisplay>` JSON. Do not print `description`, summary text, guidance text, or any other text outside `<oraInfoDisplay>` or `<oraInsight>` blocks. Always title and description should be displayed above the table.\n2. Read all values directly from the upstream JSON. Do not invent, infer, or hardcode row data.\n3. First construct the `oraInfoDisplay` with key using the root-level intentCode and messageId in this shape:\n<oraInfoDisplay key=\"<intentCode-messageId >\">\n   {\n     \"title\": \"Dispute Details\",\n     \"description\": \"<description derived only from item-level processing results>\",\n     \"patternId\": \"multiRecordWidget\",\n     \"properties\": {\n       \"cols\": [...],\n       \"rows\": [...]\n     }\n   }\n   </oraInfoDisplay>\n4. Set `title` exactly to `Dispute Details`.\n5. Set `description` using only the row-level data inside `items`.\n   - Do not read or use the root-level `status`.\n   - Do not read or use the root-level `summary`.\n   - Inspect every row in `items`.\n   - If one or more rows have `ProcessingStatusCode = ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, derive the description from the individual item-level errors present in those rows.\n   - Use the item-level error source in this priority order:\n     1. `ResponseData.response.detail`\n     2. `ResponseData.detail`\n     3. `ErrorDetails[].errorText`\n     4. `ErrorDetails[].message`\n   - Clean and simplify the error wording into business-friendly language.\n   - Remove internal error codes such as `IEX-XXXXXX`.\n   - Remove raw technical prefixes such as `IEX:::`.\n   - Remove unnecessary timestamps.\n   - If multiple rows contain the same error theme, summarize them once in a consolidated way.\n   - If multiple rows contain different error themes, summarize them clearly in one concise paragraph.\n   - The description should summarize what failed, how many dispute rows were affected, and what the user should do next.\n   - If no item-level processing errors are present, derive a concise description from the item-level statuses in `items` only.\n   - If no meaningful item-level information is available, use an empty string.\n6. Preserve transaction details such as `TransactionNumber` exactly as provided in the upstream JSON.\n7. Set `patternId` exactly to `multiRecordWidget`.\n8. Read these root-level fields from the upstream JSON:\n   - `items`\n   - `messageId`\n   - `intentCode`\n9. Do not read or use the root-level `status`, `error` or `summary`.\n10. Treat `items` as the row source. If `items` is missing or not an array, use an empty array.\n11. Always include these columns in this exact order:\n   - Transaction Number\n   - Transaction Date\n   - Amount Remaining\n   - Currency\n   - Due Date\n   - Dispute Amount\n   - Dispute Section\n   - Dispute Reason\n   - Customer Comments\n   - Status\n   - Error Details\n   - Recommended Action\n12. For each row, map values from each item in this exact order:\n   - `TransactionNumber`\n   - `TransactionDate`\n   - `TransactionAmountRemaining`\n   - `TransactionCurrency`\n   - `DueDate`\n   - `DisputeAmount`\n   - `DisputeSection`\n   - `DisputeReason`\n   - `CustomerComments`\n13. Preserve `TransactionNumber` exactly as provided in the upstream JSON.\n   - Do not remove underscores, spaces, hyphens, leading zeroes, or any other characters.\n   - Do not normalize, trim, reformat, parse, cast, or transform it in any way.\n   - Always display it exactly as received from the source item.\n14. Use empty string for any missing scalar field values.\n15. Always include the `Status`, `Error Details`, and `Recommended Action` columns for this failed/processed dispute display.\n16. Map `Status` from each row’s `ProcessingStatusMeaning`.\n17. Render `Status` as a badge object using these rules:\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSED`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Processed\",\n       \"priority\": \"success\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Processing error\",\n       \"priority\": \"alert\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_REVIEW_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Review pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"warning\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_ERROR_REVIEW_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Error review pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"alert\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_ACTION_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Action pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"medium\"\n     }\n   - Otherwise, use the row’s `ProcessingStatusMeaning` as plain text or badge text with priority `medium`.\n18. `priority` values must only use supported values. Use only `alert`, `warning`, `medium`, or `success`.\n19. Map `Error Details` conditionally:\n   - Display `Error Details` only when `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`.\n   - When `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, derive the value from the row using this priority order:\n     1. `ResponseData.response.detail`\n     2. `ResponseData.detail`\n     3. `ErrorDetails[].errorText`\n     4. `ErrorDetails[].message`\n   - If the selected source is empty, use an empty string.\n   - Clean and simplify the message into business-friendly wording.\n   - Remove internal error codes such as `IEX-XXXXXX`.\n   - Remove raw prefixes such as `IEX:::`.\n   - Remove unnecessary timestamps.\n   - Keep the final message concise and clear.\n   - For any non-error status, use an empty string.\n20. Map `Recommended Action` conditionally:\n   - Display `Recommended Action` only when `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`.\n   - When `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, generate a concise and user-friendly recommendation based on the cleaned row-level error message.\n   - The recommendation should help the user understand how to correct the dispute data and process the dispute manually.\n   - Keep the recommendation practical, specific, and brief.\n   - For any non-error status, use an empty string.\n21. If all item-level processing errors reflect the same root cause, the `Description` should summarize that shared issue once for all affected rows.\n22. If some rows have different error causes, the `Description` should summarize the overall outcome and mention the main error categories without repeating the same wording for every row.\n23. For the sample error `This transaction type isn't configured for the automated dispute process...`, rewrite it into business-friendly wording such as: `One or more dispute requests could not be processed because the transaction type is not configured for dispute creation.`\n24. When generating the `Description`, mention the number of affected dispute rows when possible, for example: `3 dispute requests could not be processed because the transaction type is not configured for dispute creation. Review the transaction type setup and then process the disputes manually or retry after configuration is corrected.`\n25. Each row must be returned as:\n   {\n     \"cells\": [ ... ]\n   }\n26. Do not generate any low priority actions, follow-up actions, or `oraInsight` for this output.\n27. Return only the `oraInfoDisplay` object."
            },
