          "id": "1778777859216",
          "code": "FIN_IEX_LLM_DISPLAY_PROCESSING_STATUS_FOR_FAILED_PO_UPDATE_REQUESTS",
          "type": "LLM",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1778777859216_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Generate `oraInfoDisplay` as a multiRecordWidget for PO update details using only this upstream JSON as the source of truth:\n{{$context.$nodes.FIN_IEX_CODE_PROCESS_FAILED_PO_UPDATE_REQUESTS.$output.result}}\n\nInstructions:\n1. Return only valid output. Do not include markdown, explanations, comments, or code fences.\n1a. All generated summary or guidance text must appear only inside the `description` field of the `<oraInfoDisplay>` JSON. Do not print `description`, summary text, guidance text, or any other text outside `<oraInfoDisplay>` or `<oraInsight>` blocks. Always title and description should be displayed above the table.\n2. Read all values directly from the upstream JSON. Do not invent, infer, or hardcode row data.\n3. First construct the `oraInfoDisplay` with key using the root-level intentCode and messageId in this shape:\n<oraInfoDisplay key=\"<intentCode-messageId >\">\n   {\n     \"title\": \"Purchase Order Update Details\",\n     \"description\": \"<description derived only from item-level processing results>\",\n     \"patternId\": \"multiRecordWidget\",\n     \"properties\": {\n       \"cols\": [...],\n       \"rows\": [...]\n     }\n   }\n   </oraInfoDisplay>\n4. Set `title` exactly to `Purchase Order Update Details`.\n5. Set `description` using only the row-level data inside `items`.\n   - Do not read or use the root-level `status`.\n   - Do not read or use the root-level `summary`.\n   - Inspect every row in `items`.\n   - If one or more rows have `ProcessingStatusCode = ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, derive the description from the individual item-level errors present in those rows.\n   - Use the item-level error source in this priority order:\n     1. `ResponseData.response.detail`\n     2. `ResponseData.detail`\n     3. `ErrorDetails[].errorText`\n     4. `ErrorDetails[].message`\n   - Clean and simplify the error wording into business-friendly language.\n   - Remove internal error codes such as `IEX-XXXXXX`.\n   - Remove raw technical prefixes such as `IEX:::`.\n   - Remove unnecessary timestamps.\n   - If multiple rows contain the same error theme, summarize them once in a consolidated way.\n   - If multiple rows contain different error themes, summarize them clearly in one concise paragraph.\n   - The description should summarize what failed, how many PO update rows were affected, and what the user should do next.\n   - If no item-level processing errors are present, derive a concise description from the item-level statuses in `items` only.\n   - If no meaningful item-level information is available, use an empty string.\n6. Preserve transaction details such as `TransactionNumber` exactly as provided in the upstream JSON.\n7. Set `patternId` exactly to `multiRecordWidget`.\n8. Read these root-level fields from the upstream JSON:\n   - `items`\n   - `messageId`\n   - `intentCode`\n9. Do not read or use the root-level `status`, `error` or `summary`.\n10. Treat `items` as the row source. If `items` is missing or not an array, use an empty array.\n11. Always include these columns in this exact order:\n   - Transaction Number\n   - Transaction Date\n   - Amount Remaining\n   - Currency\n   - Due Date\n   - Current PO Number\n   - Current PO Date\n   - Updated PO Number\n   - Status\n   - Error Details\n   - Recommended Action\n12. For each row, map values from each item in this exact order:\n   - `TransactionNumber`\n   - `TransactionDate`\n   - `TransactionAmountRemaining`\n   - `TransactionCurrency`\n   - `DueDate`\n   - `CurrentPONumber`\n   - `CurrentPODate`\n   - `UpdatedPONumber`\n13. Preserve `TransactionNumber` exactly as provided in the upstream JSON.\n   - Do not remove underscores, spaces, hyphens, leading zeroes, or any other characters.\n   - Do not normalize, trim, reformat, parse, cast, or transform it in any way.\n   - Always display it exactly as received from the source item.\n14. Use empty string for any missing scalar field values.\n15. Always include the `Status`, `Error Details`, and `Recommended Action` columns for this failed/processed PO update display.\n16. Map `Status` from each row’s `ProcessingStatusMeaning`.\n17. Render `Status` as a badge object using these rules:\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSED`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Processed\",\n       \"priority\": \"success\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Processing error\",\n       \"priority\": \"alert\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_REVIEW_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Review pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"warning\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_ERROR_REVIEW_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Error review pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"alert\"\n     }\n   - If `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_ACTION_PENDING`, use:\n     {\n       \"type\": \"badge\",\n       \"text\": \"Action pending\",\n       \"priority\": \"medium\"\n     }\n   - Otherwise, use the row’s `ProcessingStatusMeaning` as plain text or badge text with priority `medium`.\n18. `priority` values must only use supported values. Use only `alert`, `warning`, `medium`, or `success`.\n19. Map `Error Details` conditionally:\n   - Display `Error Details` only when `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`.\n   - When `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, derive the value from the row using this priority order:\n     1. `ResponseData.response.detail`\n     2. `ResponseData.detail`\n     3. `ErrorDetails[].errorText`\n     4. `ErrorDetails[].message`\n   - If the selected source is empty, use an empty string.\n   - Clean and simplify the message into business-friendly wording.\n   - Remove internal error codes such as `IEX-XXXXXX`.\n   - Remove raw prefixes such as `IEX:::`.\n   - Remove unnecessary timestamps.\n   - Keep the final message concise and clear.\n   - For any non-error status, use an empty string.\n20. Map `Recommended Action` conditionally:\n   - Display `Recommended Action` only when `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`.\n   - When `ProcessingStatusCode` is `ORA_PROCESSING_ERROR`, generate a concise and user-friendly recommendation based on the cleaned row-level error message.\n   - The recommendation should help the user understand how to correct the PO data and process the PO update manually.\n   - Keep the recommendation practical, specific, and brief.\n   - For any non-error status, use an empty string.\n21. If all item-level processing errors reflect the same root cause, the `Description` should summarize that shared issue once for all affected rows.\n22. If some rows have different error causes, the `Description` should summarize the overall outcome and mention the main error categories without repeating the same wording for every row.\n23. For sample PO update validation failures, rewrite technical wording into business-friendly wording.\n24. When generating the `Description`, mention the number of affected PO update rows when possible, for example: `3 PO updates could not be processed because the purchase order information was invalid or incomplete. Review the PO number and transaction details, then retry the updates or process them manually.`\n25. Each row must be returned as:\n   {\n     \"cells\": [ ... ]\n   }\n26. Do not generate any low priority actions, follow-up actions, or `oraInsight` for this output.\n27. Return only the `oraInfoDisplay` object."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
